@@ PE1 | Predictive
An automotive plant is retooling a production line to improve quality. Rumours spread among the team that the project is really a cost-cutting exercise that will eliminate jobs. What should the project manager do?
A) Communicate the real objectives openly and invite questions
B) Ignore the rumours, since they will disappear once work starts
C) Ask the plant manager to forbid discussion of the rumours on site
D) Promise the team that no job will be affected by the new line
= A
? A shared vision requires honest communication. Addressing the rumours openly, explaining the objectives and listening to concerns builds trust. Forbidding discussion or making promises the PM cannot keep damages credibility.

@@ PE1 | Agile
The developers complete most of their stories each sprint, but the sprint goals are rarely met because the selected stories are unrelated. What should the scrum master do?
A) Coach the team to plan around a coherent sprint goal first
B) Drop sprint goals, since completing stories is what really matters to the business
C) Ask the product owner to select all of the stories for the team before planning starts
D) Increase the number of stories in each sprint so that more of them relate to the goal
= A
? The sprint goal gives the sprint coherence and focus. Selecting work that supports one goal improves value delivery and teamwork.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
In an insurance company, the claims department and the IT department measure "claim processing time" differently, and they disagree about whether the new system is improving it. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate agreement on one definition and baseline of the metric
B) Use the IT department's definition, since IT owns the system being implemented
C) Report both definitions and let the sponsor choose the more favourable one each month
D) Stop measuring processing time, since the departments cannot agree on what it means
= A
? A common understanding of success requires shared definitions of key measures and a baseline. Competing definitions undermine alignment and trust in results.

@@ PE1 | Agile
The product owner has written a 30-page product vision document that nobody on the team has read. What would better communicate the vision?
A) A short, visual vision statement that the team creates and reviews together
B) A requirement that each developer reads the document and signs to confirm it
C) A one-hour presentation of the document's contents at every sprint review
D) Emailing the document again with a reminder about its importance to the team
= A
? A vision is most useful when it is concise, memorable and co-created, such as a vision board or elevator pitch, rather than a long document.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
A mining expansion team includes geologists, engineers and environmental specialists, each focused on their own priorities. What should the project manager do at the start?
A) Agree shared objectives, including environmental and community commitments
B) Let the engineers set the priorities, since they will build the expansion itself
C) Hold separate planning meetings for each discipline to avoid any conflict
D) Ask the environmental specialists to adjust their priorities to the engineering plan
= A
? Shared objectives that integrate technical, environmental and community commitments create a common direction. Siloed planning creates conflict later.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A new team member asks how her test automation work fits into the overall project. What should the project manager do?
A) Show how her work links to the release goals, milestones and outcomes
B) Tell her that the overall project is not something she needs to understand
C) Send her the complete project management plan and ask her to read it alone
D) Ask her team lead to explain the project when the lead has some spare time
= A
? Connecting individual work to goals and outcomes builds motivation and helps people make better decisions.

@@ PE1 | Agile | match
Match each Scrum artifact (or team agreement) with the commitment that gives it focus.
1) Product backlog
2) Sprint backlog
3) Increment
4) Team charter
A) Product goal
B) Sprint goal
C) Definition of done
D) Team values and working agreements
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? In Scrum, the product backlog commits to the product goal, the sprint backlog to the sprint goal and the increment to the definition of done. A team charter captures the team's values and working agreements.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
On a film production, the director wants an expensive additional scene, while the producer insists on staying within budget. Their argument is delaying the schedule. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion of options within the budget, escalating if needed
B) Support the director, since the creative vision determines the film's success
C) Support the producer, since the budget is fixed and cannot be discussed at all
D) Postpone the decision until the end of filming and then review the remaining budget
= A
? Collaborative problem-solving within constraints (for example, alternative ways to shoot the scene) can satisfy both needs; escalation to the executive producer is the fallback.

@@ PE2 | Agile
After a failed sprint, the front-end and back-end developers blame each other for the integration problems. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate a retrospective focused on the process, not on individuals
B) Identify which group caused the problem and ask them to fix it in the next sprint
C) Separate the front-end and back-end developers into two independent teams
D) Report the conflict to the developers' managers and ask them to intervene
= A
? Blame stops learning. A process-focused retrospective helps the team find systemic causes and agree on improvements such as earlier integration.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
The agile team urgently needs a software tool, but the procurement department's standard process takes six weeks. Tension between the two groups is rising. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with procurement to find a compliant faster route
B) Ask the team to buy the tool with a personal credit card and claim it as an expense
C) Tell the team to wait six weeks, since procurement rules cannot be changed for anyone
D) Escalate to the CEO and ask for the procurement department to make an exception
= A
? Collaborating with procurement can uncover compliant options (framework agreements, low-value thresholds). Bypassing controls or escalating immediately are inappropriate.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two equally influential stakeholders cannot agree on the layout of a new facility. Collaboration has not worked, and a decision is needed by tomorrow to avoid delaying construction. Which approach is most appropriate?
A) Compromise
B) Withdrawal
C) Smoothing
D) Forcing
= A
? When collaboration fails, time is short and the parties have equal power, compromise offers a workable solution in which each side gives up something.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A developer refuses to follow the coding standards the team agreed on, and other members are frustrated. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Talk privately with the developer to understand the reasons
B) Ask the team to remove the developer's code from the repository each time
C) Report the developer to their manager for refusing to follow team rules
D) Change the coding standards so that they match the developer's own style
= A
? A private conversation reveals the reasons — perhaps the standard has a flaw or the developer has a concern — and is the respectful first step before involving others.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The project manager has been postponing a difficult conversation with a team lead whose decisions are causing friction in the team. The friction is growing. What should the project manager do?
A) Address the issue directly with the team lead, focusing on specific behaviours and impacts
B) Continue to wait, since the friction may resolve itself as the team lead gains experience
C) Ask other team members to talk to the team lead on the project manager's behalf
D) Reassign the team lead's responsibilities quietly without discussing the reasons
= A
? Avoiding conflict lets it escalate. Addressing it directly, privately and with specifics is the most effective approach.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
The project has two co-sponsors from different business units who disagree about the priority of two major features. The team is receiving conflicting instructions. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a joint decision using the business case, escalating if needed
B) Follow the instructions of the co-sponsor whose business unit provides the most funding
C) Alternate between the two sponsors' priorities from one sprint or phase to the next
D) Let the team decide which feature to build first, since the sponsors cannot agree
= A
? The business case provides an objective basis; if the co-sponsors still disagree, governance (the steering committee) decides. Conflicting instructions must not continue.

@@ PE2 | Agile | multi
A new team is being formed. Which two actions are most likely to prevent destructive conflicts later? (Choose two.)
A) Agreeing a team charter that includes a conflict resolution process
B) Clarifying roles and responsibilities early
C) Discouraging any debate about technical approaches
D) Letting a manager make every decision for the team
E) Assigning team members to work separately from each other
= A, B
? Clear agreements and roles reduce misunderstandings and give the team a way to handle disagreements constructively. Suppressing debate or isolating people tends to create hidden conflict.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The scrum master notices that a developer commits code late every night and looks exhausted. What should the scrum master do?
A) Talk privately with the developer about workload and well-being
B) Praise the developer publicly for the extra commitment to the team
C) Ask the developer to commit code only during normal working hours
D) Wait to see whether the developer's performance starts to decline
= A
? A caring, private conversation can uncover overload or personal issues; the team can then rebalance work and protect a sustainable pace.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A team member asks the project manager for feedback on her work. How should the project manager provide it?
A) Give timely, specific feedback on strengths and areas to improve
B) Wait for the annual review so that feedback is consistent for everyone
C) Give general praise to keep her motivated and avoid negative points
D) Ask her colleagues to give her feedback anonymously instead
= A
? Effective feedback is timely, specific, balanced and actionable.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Team members are unmotivated about building a compliance reporting module that they consider boring. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain why the module matters and rotate tasks where possible
B) Assign the module to the newest team members, since they have less choice
C) Offer a bonus only to the person who finishes the module fastest
D) Tell the team that the module must be completed regardless of their feelings
= A
? Connecting work to purpose and varying tasks support intrinsic motivation; win-lose bonuses and pressure do not.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The organisation is forming three new agile teams from a pool of 24 people. The employees ask to choose which team to join. What should the project manager do?
A) Support a facilitated self-selection within agreed constraints
B) Refuse, since team composition must always be decided by managers
C) Let people choose freely without any constraints or facilitation
D) Assign people randomly so that nobody can complain about bias
= A
? Facilitated self-selection, with constraints such as skill balance, increases ownership and team cohesion.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A new team member from a culture with a strong respect for hierarchy never questions the project manager's decisions, even when he has concerns. What should the project manager do?
A) Create safe one-to-one opportunities for him to share concerns
B) Tell him in front of the team that he must challenge decisions more often
C) Assume that he agrees with every decision, since he does not object
D) Exclude him from decision-making meetings to avoid making him uncomfortable
= A
? Cultural awareness means adapting how input is sought so everyone can contribute safely.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team asks the scrum master to tell them how to solve a recurring deployment problem. What should the scrum master do?
A) Ask questions that help the team analyse and solve the problem itself
B) Give the team the solution, since the scrum master has seen this problem before
C) Ask the product owner to decide how the deployment should be done
D) Hire an external consultant to solve the deployment problem for the team
= A
? A coaching stance develops the team's capability and ownership; the scrum master guides rather than solves.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
A functional manager asks the project manager for input on a team member's annual performance review. What should the project manager provide?
A) Fair, factual feedback on observed performance
B) A vague positive comment to avoid influencing the review
C) A refusal, since performance reviews are not the PM's concern
D) A comparison ranking the team member against all colleagues
= A
? In matrix organisations, PMs contribute objective, evidence-based input to performance reviews.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Which source of power is most sustainable for a project manager who wants long-term commitment from the team?
A) Referent power based on trust and respect
B) Coercive power based on penalties
C) Reward power based on bonuses
D) Formal power based on position only
= A
? Referent and expert power build lasting commitment; coercive power damages relationships, and reward or formal power alone yields compliance rather than commitment.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
Which two observations suggest that a team has reached the norming stage? (Choose two.)
A) The team has agreed on working norms and follows them
B) Members increasingly trust each other and share information
C) Frequent disputes arise about roles and responsibilities
D) Members are polite but unsure about the project's goals
E) The team is being disbanded after completing its work
= A, B
? Norming teams adopt shared norms and build trust. Disputes about roles indicate storming; polite uncertainty indicates forming; disbanding is adjourning.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
An environmental organisation publicly criticises the mining expansion. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the organisation, listen to its concerns and share relevant data
B) Ignore the organisation, since it has no legal authority over the project
C) Ask the legal department to threaten the organisation with legal action
D) Publish a statement dismissing the organisation's criticism as uninformed
= A
? Respectful engagement with influential critics can reduce opposition and improve environmental outcomes; dismissal tends to escalate conflict.

@@ PE4 | Agile
Insurance agents, the system's main users, say they are too busy to give feedback during development. What should the product owner do?
A) Offer short, convenient feedback sessions, such as visits during their work
B) Proceed without feedback from the agents until the system is released to them
C) Ask the agents' managers to provide feedback on the agents' behalf instead
D) Ask the agents to attend the full sprint review every two weeks without exception
= A
? Adapting engagement to users' constraints keeps feedback flowing; proxies without real users risk building the wrong thing.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The system will be rolled out to the company's subsidiary in another country. The stakeholder register does not include that country's regulators or works council. What should the project manager do?
A) Identify and analyse these stakeholders and add them to the register
B) Ask the subsidiary to handle its local stakeholders on its own
C) Ignore them, since the project is managed from the head office
D) Wait until the rollout starts and see who raises concerns
= A
? International rollouts introduce new stakeholders with significant influence; they must be identified and engaged early.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Stakeholder analysis shows that three high-power stakeholders are resistant to the project. What should the project manager do?
A) Prioritise targeted engagement to understand and address their concerns
B) Focus on supportive stakeholders and avoid the resistant ones
C) Ask the sponsor to remove the resistant stakeholders from the project
D) Record the resistance in the register and take no further action
= A
? High-power resisters can block the project; they require priority, targeted engagement.

@@ PE4 | Agile
At a media company, the editorial staff who will use a new content system rarely interact with the developers building it. Misunderstandings are frequent. What should the project manager do?
A) Involve editors directly, for example in refinement and reviews
B) Ask the editors to write a complete requirements document before development continues
C) Let the developers build the system according to their own understanding of editing
D) Ask the IT manager to collect the editors' feedback once per quarter
= A
? Close, frequent collaboration between users and developers reduces misunderstandings and builds the right product.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A new ordering system will change how the company's suppliers submit invoices, affecting their staff. How should the project manager treat the suppliers?
A) As stakeholders to be identified, informed and engaged early
B) As external parties who must simply follow the new process
C) As stakeholders only if they complain about the change
D) As part of the procurement team's responsibilities only
= A
? Anyone affected by the project is a stakeholder; early engagement eases adoption and reduces resistance.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Stakeholders complain that they receive too many surveys and meeting requests from several project teams. What should the project manager do?
A) Coordinate engagement across teams to reduce the burden on stakeholders
B) Stop all surveys and meetings with stakeholders until the complaints stop
C) Continue as planned, since engagement is always beneficial for the project
D) Ask stakeholders to choose which teams they want to work with
= A
? Engagement should be effective, not excessive. Coordinating requests respects stakeholders' time and preserves goodwill.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The sponsor asks for a weekly one-to-one status meeting, but the project manager believes the sponsor would benefit more from attending sprint reviews. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the options with the sponsor and agree on the most useful format
B) Refuse the one-to-one meetings and insist that the sponsor attends reviews
C) Accept the weekly meetings and never mention the sprint reviews
D) Ask the product owner to meet the sponsor instead of the project manager
= A
? Engagement approaches should be agreed with the stakeholder, balancing their preferences with what works best.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
An automotive customer expects production to start two months earlier than the plan allows. What should the project manager do?
A) Show the critical path analysis and discuss options with their costs and risks
B) Agree to the earlier date and ask the team to find a way to achieve it
C) Refuse, since the schedule baseline was approved by the steering committee
